Settings control how NAHPU works on this device: catalog formats, controlled
lists, custom fields, presets, and app preferences. Open `Settings` from the
home screen menu or from a project's Dashboard menu. Custom fields for one
project need that project open.

## What each setting affects

| Kind | Examples | Transfer behavior |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Project information | Name, accession, location, time zone, dates, project field-ID values | Included in project-info and project transfers |
| Shared database records | Taxa and personnel | Stored in the NAHPU database and associated with projects |
| User configs | Controlled vocabularies and export or document presets | Transfer with `Export user configs` and `Import user configs` |
| Installation state | Theme, language, and current main taxon | Remains on the current installation |

Changing a setting while one project is open can affect other projects on the
same installation. Review transferred user configs before replacing your
current configuration.

## Catalogs

### Format

`Format` {% ref id="A" /%} selects the main taxon used for new specimen forms
on this device. A project can contain more than one taxon group; this setting
only controls the currently active specimen format and its measurement fields.

### Sites

`Sites` {% ref id="B" /%} manages:

- the site types and habitat types offered on site forms;
- coordinate datums;
- `Geography fields`: which geography fields site forms show;
- `Map layers` for the coordinate map.

Hiding a field never deletes values already stored in it.

### Events

`Events` {% ref id="C" /%} manages the primary-activity list {% ref id="G" /%},
collection methods, event-personnel roles, and the environmental fields shown
on event forms. Primary activities default to `Collecting`, `Recording`, and
`Observing`; `Other` is not included unless you add it. Environmental forms
start with ambient temperature, ambient humidity, cloud cover, rainfall, and
notes; show or hide other fields the same way as geography fields.

Keep these lists the same on every field device by transferring user configs.

### Specimens

`Specimens` {% ref id="D" /%} contains:

- `Always show collector field` and other capture-field visibility;
- `Field ID` and `Tissue ID` numbering, described below;
- controlled lists for specimen sex, `Identification Methods`, `Life Stages`,
  specimen types, treatments, and conditions;
- symbiont settings: interaction types, categories, detection and preparation
  methods, anatomical locations, storage, and treatments, all starting with
  editable defaults.

The specimen-sex list is restricted. `Male`, `Female`, and `Unknown` are always
available. Use the `+` chips to add `Gynandromorph`, `Hermaphrodite`,
`Female?`, or `Male?`. An added option can be removed only when no specimen in
the database uses it.

#### Field ID

Under `Field ID`, choose how specimens are numbered:

| Choice | Field ID is built from | Needs |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Personnel ID` (default) | The Cataloger's initials and their own running number | A person with the `Cataloger` role in the project, with initials and a current field number |
| `Project ID` | `Prefix` + `Current catalog number` + `Suffix` | The three values, and whether `Auto-increment catalog number` is on |

With `Project ID`, NAHPU joins the three values directly: `NAHPU-`, `120`, and
`-M` give `NAHPU-120-M`. Select `Save field ID settings` to apply them.

{% aside type="caution" %}

Changing a project-ID prefix or suffix changes how assigned project IDs are
displayed, and NAHPU asks you to confirm. Check first whether those IDs are
already exported or written on labels.

{% /aside %}

#### Tissue ID

`Tissue ID` takes a prefix, a next number, and a suffix, and previews the next
tissue ID. Leading zeros are kept, so `M0042-T` is followed by `M0043-T`. For
the Ichthyology catalog format, `Individual tag ID` numbers the individuals in a
fish lot the same way; see
[Fishes](/en/usages/specimens/measurements/fishes/#tag-id-numbering).

### Custom fields {% #custom-fields %}

`Custom fields` {% ref id="E" /%} adds your own fields to site, environmental,
specimen, part, and symbiont forms.

| Setting | Options |
| --- | --- |
| Type | Text, Number, Yes / No, Dropdown |
| Scope | `Current project` (this project only) or `Global` (every project on this installation); chosen at creation |
| Catalog | Specimen-related fields can be limited to some catalog formats |
| Darwin Core mapping | Optional export term for the field |

{% steps %}

1. Open the project, go to `Settings` > `Custom fields`, and select
   `Add custom field`. Choose where the field appears.
2. Set `Label` and `Type`. For a dropdown, enter the allowed options. Put the
   unit in the label of a number field, such as "Canopy height (m)".
3. Choose the scope, and for specimen fields, the catalog formats.
4. Optionally set a Darwin Core mapping. Mapping a field does not check its
   values, so inspect a sample export before sharing data.
5. Save, open a record in that section, enter a test value, and reopen the
   record to check it.
6. To share the field, export it from `Custom fields` or with the user configs,
   import it on the other device, and check the section, scope, and options.

{% /steps %}

To stop using a field, select `Archive`: its data stays, and `Restore` brings it
back. `Delete` shows as `Delete (values in use)` and is unavailable while any
record stores a value. Discarding legacy values is a separate, irreversible
action.

## Database

- `Taxa` {% ref id="F" /%} manages taxonomy records available to projects.
- `Personnel` manages people in the database and their specimen-care roles.
- `Backup database` saves a full ZIP or TAR.GZ archive of every project, record,
  and managed file.
- `Replace database` replaces the active SQLite database from a backup.

{% aside type="caution" %}

Make a current backup before using `Replace database`. Replacing the database
is different from merging a project or importing user configs.

{% /aside %}

## Exports

- `Tabular Data` manages reusable record-export presets.
- `Documents` manages document templates, print-layout presets, and the
  fonts available to templates.
- `Template Editor` creates and edits document templates.

`Load defaults` in the options menu, or in an empty preset list, lets you choose
which bundled presets to add. Presets that suit any catalog format start
checked; presets written for one catalog format start unchecked and are also
offered in `Setup NAHPU`. Loading never overwrites a preset of the same name, and
any preset can be deleted.

These presets are user configs. Export them when another installation must
reproduce the same columns, labels, or layouts.

## Applications

These preferences apply only to this installation.

| Setting | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `Theme` | `System`, `Light`, or `Dark`. |
| `Language` | The app's language. In beta. |
| `Regional format` | How dates and numbers are shown on screen. Saved records and exports keep standard formats. In beta. |
| `Tools` | Chooses the tools shown on the project dashboard. |
| `Data usage` | Storage report and cleanup, described below. |

### Data usage and cleanup

Open `Data usage` to scan the folders managed by NAHPU. The summary reports
total storage, file and media counts, and space that can be reclaimed from
unlinked files.

`Remove unlinked files` deletes files that are no longer used by any record,
including leftovers from deleted media or cancelled imports. Linked files,
databases and backups, custom fonts, map layers, and files outside
NAHPU-managed folders are not affected. Remove an installed font from
`Documents` > `Fonts` rather than from here.

The `Files` tree lets you inspect stored files, save a copy, or remove eligible
files one by one. Choose `Select` to work with several files. Use `Clear` or
`Select all`, then choose the floating `Review` button to check the file count,
size, and locations before exporting an archive or permanently removing the
files.

Choose `Rescan` after files have changed outside NAHPU to refresh the report.

## Transferring user configs

`Export user configs` is in the home screen menu and the project Dashboard
menu; `Import user configs` is in the Dashboard menu. Neither is inside the
Settings page. A user-config transfer reproduces controlled lists and presets
without replacing the project database. See [Export](../export) before
replacing configs or data.
